A function $f$ on $X$ is a triple $(f(a), f(b), f(c))$. The integral against $\mu$ is
$$\int_X |f|\,d\mu = |f(a)|\cdot\mu(\{a\}) + |f(b)|\cdot\mu(\{b\}) + |f(c)|\cdot\mu(\{c\}) = |f(a)|\cdot 0 + |f(b)|\cdot 1 + |f(c)|\cdot\infty.$$
Two functions are identified in every $L^p$ space when they agree $\mu$-a.e. The only $\mu$-null set is $\varnothing$ together with $\{a\}$ (and subsets thereof), since $\mu(\{b\})=1>0$ and $\mu(\{c\})=\infty>0$. Thus the value $f(a)$ is **irrelevant** in every $L^p(\mu)$; only $(f(b),f(c))$ matters.

**$L^1(\mu)$.** We need $\int|f|\,d\mu<\infty$, i.e. $|f(b)| + |f(c)|\cdot\infty <\infty$. This forces $f(c)=0$ (with the convention $0\cdot\infty=0$ in integration), while $f(b)$ is arbitrary and $f(a)$ is irrelevant. Hence
$$L^1(\mu) \cong \{(f(b),f(c)) : f(c)=0\} \cong \mathbb{R},$$
so $\dim L^1(\mu) = 1$.

**$L^2(\mu)$.** We need $\int|f|^2\,d\mu = |f(a)|^2\cdot 0 + |f(b)|^2\cdot 1 + |f(c)|^2\cdot\infty <\infty$, which again forces $f(c)=0$, with $f(b)$ arbitrary and $f(a)$ irrelevant. Thus
$$L^2(\mu) \cong \mathbb{R},$$
so $\dim L^2(\mu) = 1$.

**$L^\infty(\mu)$.** A function is essentially bounded when $\mu(\{x:|f(x)|>M\})=0$ for some $M<\infty$. Since $\{b\}$ and $\{c\}$ both have positive measure, any such level set $\{|f|>M\}$ cannot contain $b$ or $c$; it may only be contained in $\{a\}$. Therefore $|f(b)|\le M$ and $|f(c)|\le M$ are required, while $f(a)$ is unconstrained (and irrelevant a.e.). The essential supremum is $\operatorname{ess\,sup}|f|=\max(|f(b)|,|f(c)|)$, which is finite iff both $|f(b)|,|f(c)|$ are finite. Hence
$$L^\infty(\mu) \cong \mathbb{R}^2,$$
so $\dim L^\infty(\mu) = 2$.

**Answers:** $\dim L^1(\mu)=1$, $\dim L^2(\mu)=1$, $\dim L^\infty(\mu)=2$.

The key mechanism is that the atom $\{c\}$ of infinite measure excludes any nonzero value there from the integrable (and square-integrable) functions but not from the essentially bounded ones, while the null atom $\{a\}$ is quotiented out in every $L^p$ space.

